Right — literal human reproductive mechanism first.

The closest human analog to fruit is the offspring, and the closest analog to plant grafting is interesting because humans solve the same biological problem differently.

A fruiting plant packages an embryo/seed with resources and structures that improve dispersal/survival. Humans package the developing offspring internally:

\text{fertilization}
\rightarrow
\text{embryo}
\rightarrow
\text{implantation}
\rightarrow
\text{placenta}
\rightarrow
\text{maternal resource exchange}
\rightarrow
\text{birth}

The particularly interesting part relative to our graft discussion is the placenta.

The fetus is genetically distinct from the mother—half its nuclear genome ordinarily comes from the father—yet it establishes an intimate physiological interface with maternal tissue. The immune system therefore faces a version of the compatibility problem we were discussing: permit a partly foreign organism to develop without treating it like an ordinary incompatible graft, while still maintaining immune defense.

So nature doesn’t merely answer:

compatible / incompatible.

It constructs an interface that regulates exchange.

Placental tissues mediate oxygen, nutrients, waste, hormones and immune interactions while maternal and fetal blood normally remain in separate circulations.

That’s much closer to our Callus idea than the metaphor initially suggested:

\text{Mother}
\leftrightarrow
\boxed{\text{Placental interface}}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Fetus}

And then birth deliberately breaks that interface once the offspring can transition to another support system.

So the general mechanism is beautifully concrete:

foreignness → controlled interface → selective exchange → protected development → increasing independence → separation.

That’s a useful biological primitive without needing to push it into abstraction yet.